hi I'm Evan Carmichael and welcome to  another edition of Molyneux masters I  believe that the fastest and most  effective way to grow a business is to  model the strategies of people who have  already done what you're trying to do so  today we're gonna look at how a high  school dropout who was always in trouble  with the law  turns his life around by believing in  himself he went on to become a two-time  world boxing champion and a  multi-millionaire entrepreneur this is a  story of boxing great George Foreman and  a top three lessons that you can learn  from his success  George big George Foreman is an Olympic  gold medalist  two-time former world heavyweight boxing  champion in entrepreneur a high school  dropout  as a teenager Foreman didn't know how to  read or write he took to the streets and  got in the trouble by picking pockets  and mugging people his sister told him  you'll never be anything after finding  himself on the run from the police one  night and digging himself into a pile of  mud to avoid their dogs finding him  Foreman decided he had to change his  life he enrolled in a job skills  training program for youth and began  learning how to read and write  he later also learned how to box and  entered into amateur competitions so he  could send fifty dollars home to his  mother every month after a year and a  half of learning the Box he entered into  the Olympic Games and won the gold medal  he went on to win the world heavyweight  boxing championship twice after twice  retiring from professional boxing  Foreman became an entrepreneur and  toured the world selling George Foreman  grills in 2002 alone the company earned  922 million dollars from selling the  grills Foreman would go on to earn three  times more money from the grills than  throat his entire career as a boxer he  has also moved on to selling TV shows  clothing books and IndyCar racing teams  even though nobody thought he'd ever  accomplished anything in his life George  Foreman turned his life around by  believing in himself and went on to  become a champion boxer and entrepreneur  to help you turn your business into a  success  here are three auction items and you can  learn from George Foreman auction item  number one learn how to sell if you want  to be a successful entrepreneur you need  to develop the ability to sell selling  isn't just convincing customers to buy  our products or services selling is  getting employees media investors  suppliers and everyone else was involved  in your business to believe in what  you're doing it starts with a strong  passion for what you're creating if you  love what you're doing I think you can  really help people it's easy to get  people excited about it and draw others  in if you don't really believe in what  you're doing why should others believe  in you after Foreman came back to boxing  and at 44 years of age became the oldest  person ever to become a champion he  started getting calls to endorse  companies and become their spokesman  when they saw he could sell anything  from food appliances to cleaning  products the companies lined up to get  him to represent them from Doritos to  Kentucky Fried Chicken to McDonald's  Foreman went to work selling her  products and people listened according  the foreman in time I learned the  importance of selling I love selling if  you learn the sell it's worth more than  a degree it's with more than the  heavyweight championship of the world  it's even more important than having a  million dollars in the bank learn to  sell and you'll never starve  auction auto number to get people to  like you people like buying from people  they like the more you can get your  customers to like you as a person the  more likely it is they're gonna buy from  you people also prefer Bynes than people  rather than corporations make sure you  show your clients to history and the  people behind your company talk about  how and why you started the business and  the impact that you're having the more  personal you can make it the more people  are gonna like you and the more they're  gonna want to buy from you during his  early boxing career people hated George  Foreman inside and outside of the ring  he stormed into the ring with rage  didn't have much finesse to his game he  was in it for the money at the start and  didn't care what other people thought of  him he even showed up the one fight  against Muhammad Ali with an angry  German Shepherd by his side to try to  intimidate his opponent but in order for  him to be an effective salesperson he  had to change his persona and get people  to like him according to Foreman I get  mad at the critics other boxers I wanted  to destroy everybody with my fists I  figure I'm gonna make a lot of money I  didn't care about nothing else but  making a lot of money I didn't care  people like me I caught off all my hair  on my mustache I didn't want to be  identified with the old guy I learned to  make people love me to get my message  across when I had to change the box  again  I saw boxing not as a sport the second  time around but is pure business which  meant making meetings and trying to make  myself attractive to Madison Avenue  action of number three keep trying until  it works it's rare that entrepreneurs  make it big off their first idea and  even if they do the idea has changed  many times from what they thought they  were gonna create failing is a part of  business the key is to get started  too many people don't start because  they're afraid of failing you will know  failure keep going  fail smarter the next time and  eventually success will be yours Foreman  is an afraid of failing in business he's  done everything from specialty shoes  made for diabetics to a health food  restaurant chain to a reality television  series he's willing to try almost  anything if he thinks it might be a good  opportunity and it's something he  believes it as long as the business is  something he can feel proud of and won't  damage his integrity Foreman is willing  to stretch his brand to do almost  anything you never know until you try  according to Foreman you've got to start  out early in the morning and look at  hundreds literally hundreds of things  and it may take a year it may take three  or four years but you're going to hit  something so you have something to put  on the table for your family you know  you put out a lot of buckshot  you're gonna strike one so remember  learn how to sell get people to like you  and keep trying until it works to finish  up this video I wanted to share one of  my favorite true stories about George  Foreman and some of his best quotes with  a criminal record and a reputation for  being a troublemaker whew gave Foreman a  chance when he joined the u.s. job corps  looking for a way to get off the streets  he caused so many problems that he was  almost expelled from the program  his supervisor advised him to find a  different outlet for his anger and  suggested he take up boxing when Foreman  first started the box he was so scared  that he just closed his eyes and swung  his massive fists to win the fights it  was only until he started going up  against better boxers that he had to  learn the fight with his eyes open  thank you so much for joining me for  another edition of Mali in the masters  if you liked the video I want to see  more please give it a thumbs up below  I'd also love to hear oh you have to  think if you wanna leave a comment  underneath the video and stay tuned for  next episode  you
 

